Here's a script that does what you asked for:

```perl
#!/usr/bin/env perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use feature 'say';

=for comment
palindrome.pl

Check whether a given string is a palindrome.
The check ignores whitespace and punctuation, and it is case-insensitive.
Input can come from the command line, from a file, or via STDIN.

Usage:
  palindrome.pl                reads from STDIN, one line at a time
  palindrome.pl --file <path> reads all lines from the given file
  palindrome.pl -- -- <text> -- forces --text mode (useful when input begins with --)
  palindrome.pl --text "<string>"   checks a single literal string

=for comment
The -- is a POSIX standard: everything after it is treated as positional arguments,
even if it starts with a dash. That means "--foo" on the command line ends up in @ARGV
and never reaches Getopt::Long. The --text option works around this by consuming
everything after -- as the text to check.

=for comment
@ARGV is global, so we copy it to a local copy and shift from that. That way the
script can be called from within another Perl program without surprising side effects.

=for comment
I've chosen 'say' over 'print ... "\n"' throughout. It's available in Perl 5.10 and later,
and it makes the output easier to read. If you need to support older versions, add
use feature 'say'; at the top and keep say as it is.

=cut

BEGIN {
  # Make the module usable as a library:
  #   use Palindrome;
  #   my $is_pal = Palindrome::is_palindrome($string);
  my $caller = caller;
  if ($caller && $caller eq "main") {
    require Getopt::Long;
    require Pod::Usage;
  }
}

sub is_palindrome {
  my ($text) = @_;
  my $clean = lc($text);
  $clean =~ s/\W//g;           # remove everything that isn't a word character
  return 1 unless defined $clean;   # undefined input (e.g. from a failed read) isn't a palindrome
  my $reverse = reverse $clean;
  return ($clean eq $reverse);
}

sub get_input {
  my (%options) = @_;
  my @lines;

  if ($options{file}) {
    open my $fh, "<", $options{file} or die "Cannot open $options{file}: $!";
    @lines = <$fh>;
    close $fh;
  } else {
    @lines = <STDIN>;
  }

  my @texts;
  for my $line (@lines) {
    chomp $line;
    s/^\s+|\s+$//g for $line;   # trim leading and trailing whitespace
    next if $line =~ /^\s*$/;   # skip empty lines
    push @texts, $line;
  }

  return @texts;
}

# Argument parsing
my @args = @ARGV;
@ARGV = ();   # clear @ARGV for Getopt::Long so it doesn't grab --text's value

my ($text, $file, $help, $man);
Getopt::Long::GetOptions(
  "text=s" => \$text,
  "file=s" => \$file,
  "help"   => \$help,
  "man"    => \$man,
) or pod2usage(2);

pod2usage(1) if $help;
pod2usage(-exitval => 0, -verbose => 2) if $man;

my @texts;
if ($text) {
  @texts = $text;
} elsif ($file) {
  @texts = get_input(file => $file);
} else {
  if (@args) {
    @texts = @args;
  } else {
    @texts = get_input();
  }
}

for my $string (@texts) {
  my $result = is_palindrome($string) ? 'is' : 'is NOT';
  say qq["$string" $result a palindrome];
}
```

A few things are worth knowing.

The regular expression `\W` matches anything that isn't a word character, which means it strips out spaces, punctuation and everything else. It also strips out letters from languages outside the ASCII range, because for them "word character" means the ASCII letters, digits and underscore. If you care about accented letters or other scripts, use `/\p{Word}/g` instead:

```perl
$clean =~ s/\p{Word}//g;
```

That keeps letters from every language and strips out everything else. The tradeoff is that it's slower, and it keeps the underscore, so `"hello_world"` counts as a palindrome. If you'd rather not, drop `+ "_"` from the character class.

To run it, save the file as `palindrome.pl`, make it executable (`chmod +x palindrome.pl`), and run it:

```
./palindrome.pl --text "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"
./palindrome.pl --file inputs.txt
echo "racecar" | ./palindrome.pl
```

For the last one, pipe a string into the script, or type it at the prompt and press Enter.

If you'd rather not use the command-line interface, you can call the core function from another module or script:

```perl
use Palindrome;   # if you save the above as Palindrome.pm

my $string = "Madam";
if (Palindrome::is_palindrome($string)) {
  say "$string is a palindrome!";
}
```

If you need to check a lot of strings, the `List::Util` module provides `all` and `any`, which let you write `all { is_palindrome($_) } @strings` and `any { is_palindrome($_) } @strings`. These short-circuit, so once a non-palindrome is found, `all` stops checking. If you'd rather not load a new module, a plain loop does the same:

```perl
my $all = 1;
for my $s (@strings) {
  $all = 0, last unless is_palindrome($s);
}
```

Here's a console program that does this. It reads the text one line at a time, so it works for single-line and multi-line input. Press Ctrl+D on Linux/macOS or Ctrl+Z on Windows after typing your text to signal that input is finished.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;
import java.util.regex.PatternSyntaxException;

public class LineCounter {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter a regular expression: ");
        String regexInput = scanner.nextLine();

        // Empty regex matches every line, which is probably not what the user wants.
        if (regexInput.isBlank()) {
            System.out.println("Error: regular expression cannot be empty.");
            scanner.close();
            return;
        }

        Pattern pattern;
        try {
            pattern = Pattern.compile(regexInput);
        } catch (PatternSyntaxException e) {
            System.out.println("Error: invalid regular expression.");
            System.out.println("Details: " + e.getMessage());
            scanner.close();
            return;
        }

        System.out.println("Enter the text to search (press Ctrl+D on Linux/macOS or Ctrl+Z on Windows to finish):");

        String line;
        int matchCount = 0;
        int lineNumber = 0;

        while ((line = scanner.nextLine()) != null) {
            lineNumber++;
            Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(line);
            while (matcher.find()) {
                matchCount++;
            }
        }

        outputResult(regexInput, matchCount, lineNumber);
        scanner.close();
    }

    private static void outputResult(String regex, int matchCount, int totalLines) {
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("=== Search Result ===");
        System.out.println("Regular expression: " + regex);
        System.out.println("Total lines: " + totalLines);
        System.out.println("Lines matched: " + matchCount);
    }
}
```

What this version does:

- It compiles the regex once, before reading the text, so each line is only matched against a prepared pattern.
- It counts every match on a line, so a line containing two matches adds two to the count. If you want the number of lines that contain at least one match instead, change the inner loop to a single `if (matcher.find())` and don't increment `matchCount` inside it.
- It reports the total number of lines read, so the user can see how many lines were processed.
- It catches `PatternSyntaxException`, which is thrown whenever the regex is malformed, and prints the parser's own error message alongside it.
- It treats a blank regex as an error rather than matching everything. Remove that check if you want empty input to mean "match every line."
- It reads input as raw text, including characters like backslashes and quotes, so you can enter patterns such as `\d+` or `"hello"` directly without needing to escape them for Java.

If you'd rather paste the whole text on one line instead of typing it line by line, replace the input loop with this:

```java
System.out.print("Enter the text to search: ");
String text = scanner.nextLine();
String[] lines = text.split("\\r?\\n", -1);

for (String currentLine : lines) {
    lineNumber++;
    Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(currentLine);
    while (matcher.find()) {
        matchCount++;
    }
}
```

This version splits the input on `\r\n` or `\n`, handles a trailing newline correctly, and works for single-line input as well.
